<article class="prose-about">

  <header class="mb-10">
    <h1 class="text-display-sm font-semibold text-greyscale-900 leading-tight">About this citator</h1>
    <p class="mt-3 text-md text-greyscale-700 leading-relaxed">
      A preview of an AI-powered citator from
      <a href="https://free.law/" class="links" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Free Law Project</a>.
      This page explains what the preview covers, how to read it, and
      <a href="#feedback" class="links">where your feedback helps most</a>.
    </p>
    <p class="about-notice">
      <strong>For the best experience, use a desktop.</strong> The preview is designed for larger screens.
    </p>
  </header>

  <section class="about-section">
    <h2>What this is</h2>
    <p>
      A citator surfaces how a court opinion has been treated by other opinions — reversed, overruled, criticized, distinguished, or simply cited. A citator is used to determine whether a case is still good law before relying on it.
    </p>
    <p class="font-semibold text-greyscale-900">
      This demo previews the AI-powered citator that Free Law Project will integrate into
      <a href="https://www.courtlistener.com/" class="links" target="_blank" rel="noopener">CourtListener</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="about-section">
    <h2>What's in this preview</h2>
    <p>
      The preview is built around <strong>{{ scope.anchors }} anchor opinions</strong>, chosen to span the court system — from the Supreme Court of the United States down to a state surrogate's court, and from {{ scope.anchor_earliest_year }} to {{ scope.anchor_latest_year }}. Around each anchor sit the cases it cites and the cases that cite it.
    </p>
    <dl class="stat-grid">
      <div class="stat-card">
        <dt>Opinions</dt>
        <dd class="stat-card__value">{{ scope.opinions_display }}</dd>
        <dd class="stat-card__note">{{ scope.anchors }} anchors, {{ scope.authorities_display }} cases they cite, and {{ scope.citing_display }} later opinions that cite an anchor. Those citing opinions come only from courts whose decisions <strong>control or equal</strong> the anchor’s own.</dd>
      </div>
      <div class="stat-card">
        <dt>Courts</dt>
        <dd class="stat-card__value">{{ scope.courts }}</dd>
        <dd class="stat-card__note">Federal and state, trial through courts of last resort.</dd>
      </div>
      <div class="stat-card">
        <dt>Authority references</dt>
        <dd class="stat-card__value">{{ scope.authority_rows_display }}</dd>
        <dd class="stat-card__note">Every case cited inside an opinion here, counted once per opinion citing it — what fills the Authorities tabs.</dd>
      </div>
      <div class="stat-card">
        <dt>Citing references</dt>
        <dd class="stat-card__value">{{ scope.citing_rows_display }}</dd>
        <dd class="stat-card__note">Times one opinion here cites another — what fills the Cited By tabs. {{ scope.citing_rows_at_anchor_display }} of them point at an anchor; the rest point at the other cases in the preview.</dd>
      </div>
    </dl>
  </section>

  <section class="about-section">
    <h2>Finding your way around</h2>

    <h3>The opinion list</h3>
    <p>
      The home page lists every opinion in the preview, newest first. Search by case name <em>or</em> by citation. Filter by court through the court picker, or by severity: a row of five toggles, most severe on the left, for each of what the opinion decided, what happened to the case on appeal, and how later courts have treated it. Selecting several shows every opinion that matches any of them. <strong>Anchor opinions only</strong> starts switched on, which narrows the list to the ten cases the preview is built around — clear it to browse everything.
    </p>
    <p>
      Each card carries up to three Citator classes: <strong>Disposition</strong>, what the court decided, in the court's own words (<em>Affirmed</em>, <em>Reversed and remanded</em>, or <em>Ordered</em> when the order is none of the named kinds); <strong>On appeal</strong>, what happened to the case as it moved through the courts; and <strong>Later courts</strong>, how later cases have treated it. A class only appears when there is something to report. Where a card shows a treatment, clicking it opens the citing opinion at its entry for this case.
    </p>

    <h3>The opinion page</h3>
    <p>Four tabs, each answering a different question.</p>
    <div class="about-cards">
      <div class="about-card">
        <strong>Opinion</strong>
        <span>The full text. Every case the opinion cites is tinted; a case with a negative treatment also carries a small dot in the colour of its most serious treatment. Click a citation and a navigator opens: step through every place that case is mentioned, open its opinion when the preview has it, or jump straight to its entry under Authorities.</span>
      </div>
      <div class="about-card">
        <strong>Authorities</strong>
        <span>Every case cited in the opinion: its name and citations, court, docket number and date, how often this opinion cites it, what this opinion does to it and what it recognizes other courts did. Ordered by how serious the treatment is; search it by name or citation, change the order, and filter by the severity of the applied treatment, of the recognized treatments, or both. Selecting several severities shows every entry that matches any of them.</span>
      </div>
      <div class="about-card">
        <strong>Cited By</strong>
        <span>Every later opinion that cites this one: its name and citations, court, docket number and date, how often it cites this case, what it does to this opinion and what it recognizes other courts did. Ordered by how serious the treatment is; search it by name or citation, change the order, and filter by the severity of the applied treatment, of the recognized treatments, or both. Selecting several severities shows every entry that matches any of them. For the anchors the list is every citing opinion from a controlling or equal court; for every other opinion it is the opinions in this preview that cite it.</span>
      </div>
      <div class="about-card">
        <strong>History</strong>
        <span>The case's route through the court system, drawn on two axes: courts along the bottom, lowest on the left and highest on the right, and time up the side, oldest at the bottom and newest at the top. An appeal moves up and to the right; a remand comes back to the left. Each card says what that court did, and opens its details.</span>
      </div>
    </div>

    <h3>Reading the treatment evidence</h3>
    <p>
      Any entry with a negative treatment expands. Inside is one card per treatment, in the same order as the pills on the entry: the treatment the citing court applied first, then the treatments it recognizes. Each card gives the treatment, the reasoning, and the passage from the citing opinion that supports it, with two checks under the passage: whether it was found in the text, and whether it names the case. <strong>Read in the opinion</strong> takes you to that passage in context; on Cited By, <strong>See it in the citing opinion</strong> opens the citing opinion at its entry for this case.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="about-section">
    <h2>How to read a treatment</h2>

    <h3>Severity tiers</h3>
    <p>Every treatment rolls up into one of these tiers, most consequential first.</p>
    <dl class="tier-list">
      <dt>{{ severityBadge("Stop", "Stop") }}</dt>
      <dd>No longer good law for the proposition cited.</dd>
      <dt>{{ severityBadge("Warning", "Warning") }}</dt>
      <dd>Partially weakened but not entirely overturned.</dd>
      <dt>{{ severityBadge("Caution", "Caution") }}</dt>
      <dd>Intact but applicability is qualified.</dd>
      <dt>{{ severityBadge("Neutral", "Neutral") }}</dt>
      <dd>Stands; citation is informational or procedural.</dd>
      <dt>{{ severityBadge("Positive", "Positive") }}</dt>
      <dd>Affirmed on appeal.</dd>
    </dl>

    <h3>Disposition, On appeal and Later courts</h3>
    <p>
      For every opinion the citator identifies what the court itself decided, what then happened to the case, and how later cases have treated it. The three answer different questions and should not be read as one signal.
    </p>
    <ul class="about-list">
      <li><strong>Disposition</strong> — what this opinion did with the case in front of it, in the past tense as the court states it: affirmed, reversed, vacated, remanded, or a mix. An order that is none of those, such as a motion decided or a petition dismissed, shows as <em>Ordered</em>, and opens the sentence that disposed of the case.</li>
      <li><strong>On appeal</strong> — what happened to this case afterwards, as it moved further through the courts. Was this decision itself affirmed, reversed, vacated, or remanded? The citator works this out from the later opinions that cite this one, so a step can only appear where a later opinion cites or names this case.</li>
      <li><strong>Later courts</strong> — what later, unrelated cases said about the rule the case established. Did they follow it, distinguish it, criticize it, or overrule it? This is the signal for whether the case is still good law in practice.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Other things to notice</h3>
    <div class="about-cards">
      <div class="about-card">
        <strong>Both sides of the same treatment</strong>
        <span>A citation is recorded once and shown from both ends: it appears under <em>Authorities</em> on the opinion that applied it, and under <em>Cited By</em> on the opinion it was applied to, with the same treatment, severity, supporting passage and citation count. The two tabs are the two sides of one table, so they can never disagree.</span>
      </div>
      <div class="about-card">
        <strong>What a court reports, versus what it does</strong>
        <span>Under the citing court's own treatment, a pill reading <em>Recognizes to be…</em> on Authorities — <em>… as recognized by</em> on Cited By — is a treatment the court is not applying itself, but reporting: how another court has already treated the case.</span>
      </div>
      <div class="about-card">
        <strong>Every treatment shows its evidence</strong>
        <span>Every treatment carries the passage from the citing opinion behind it, so you can read the court’s own words and judge the call for yourself.</span>
      </div>
      <div class="about-card">
        <strong>The stripe on an entry</strong>
        <span>Each entry on <em>Authorities</em> and <em>Cited By</em> carries a coloured stripe down its left edge showing the most serious treatment it holds — the court's own, or one it reports. An entry can read <em>Cited by</em> and still carry a red stripe, because another court’s overruling of the case was recognized. Sorting by severity orders by that most serious treatment, as applied or as recognized by the citing case.</span>
      </div>
      {% if flags.validation %}
      <div class="about-card">
        <strong>Editorial validation marks</strong>
        <span>Three icons next to each treatment indicate whether the classification has been validated against an expert's classification — Validated, Invalidated, or Unvalidated.</span>
      </div>
      {% endif %}
    </div>

    <details class="treatment-expand">
      <summary class="treatment-expand__summary">
        {{ icon("chevron-right", "w-3.5 h-3.5 expand-chevron") }}
        <span>See the full treatment list</span>
      </summary>
      <p class="mt-3 text-sm text-greyscale-600 leading-relaxed">
        Hover over or focus any treatment, here or anywhere on the site, for its definition. In the definitions, the <em>acting case</em> is the opinion applying the treatment and the <em>target case</em> is the one receiving it.
      </p>
      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table class="about-table">
          <thead>
            <tr><th class="w-1/5">Severity</th><th class="w-2/5">Disposition / On Appeal</th><th class="w-2/5">Later courts</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {% for row in treatments %}
            <tr>
              <td>{{ severityBadge(row.severity, row.severity) }}</td>
              <td><ul class="about-table__list about-table__list--pills">{% for t in row.direct_history %}<li>{{ severityBadge(row.severity, t, false, treatment_definitions[t]) }}</li>{% endfor %}</ul></td>
              <td><ul class="about-table__list about-table__list--pills">{% for t in row.citing_reference %}<li>{{ severityBadge(row.severity, t, false, treatment_definitions[t]) }}</li>{% endfor %}</ul></td>
            </tr>
            {% endfor %}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>
        Each negative treatment also has an "as recognized by" form (e.g., <em>Limited as recognized by</em>) — used when the citing court isn't doing the action itself, but is acknowledging that some other case has. It keeps the same severity tier as the underlying treatment, and sits under the court's own treatment in the Treatment column.
      </p>
    </details>
  </section>

  <section class="about-section">
    <h2>Preview vs. production</h2>
    <p>
      The preview is deliberately scoped — running it as a standalone site lets us validate the citator design and user experience, and gather expert feedback on its classifications, before committing to a full CourtListener integration.
    </p>
    <p>Several things will look different in production:</p>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table class="about-table">
        <thead>
          <tr><th class="w-1/5">Aspect</th><th class="w-2/5">In this preview</th><th class="w-2/5">In production (CourtListener)</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {% if flags.validation %}
          <tr>
            <td class="font-semibold text-greyscale-900">Validation icons</td>
            <td>
              <p class="mb-2">All three icons render — demo-only diagnostics for experts auditing the classifications:</p>
              <ul class="about-table__list about-table__list--icons">
                <li>{{ icon("check-circle", "w-4 h-4 text-blue-700") }}<span><strong>Validated</strong> — matches the expert.</span></li>
                <li>{{ icon("x-circle", "w-4 h-4 text-purple-600") }}<span><strong>Invalidated</strong> — the expert found a negative treatment that was missed or labelled differently.</span></li>
                <li>{{ icon("question-circle", "w-4 h-4 text-greyscale-500") }}<span><strong>Unvalidated</strong> — no expert review.</span></li>
              </ul>
            </td>
            <td>
              <p class="mb-2">Only the blue Validated check renders. Most cases won't have expert review, so the other two would be noise on everything listed.</p>
              <ul class="about-table__list about-table__list--icons">
                <li>{{ icon("check-circle", "w-4 h-4 text-blue-700") }}<span><strong>Validated</strong> only.</span></li>
              </ul>
            </td>
          </tr>
          {% endif %}
          <tr>
            <td class="font-semibold text-greyscale-900">Coverage</td>
            <td>{{ scope.opinions_display }} opinions around {{ scope.anchors }} anchors. Complete authorities lists for the anchors and for the opinions from controlling courts that cite them.</td>
            <td>Every opinion CourtListener holds.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="font-semibold text-greyscale-900">Links between cases</td>
            <td>A cited case opens its own page only when it is one of the {{ scope.opinions_display }} opinions here; otherwise its name is plain text.</td>
            <td>Every cited case links to its opinion.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="font-semibold text-greyscale-900">Search</td>
            <td>Case name or citation, across the opinions in the preview.</td>
            <td>Full keyword search across opinion bodies, plus semantic search.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="font-semibold text-greyscale-900">Filters</td>
            <td>Court and severity on the opinion list; search by name or citation, sorting and a severity filter over applied and recognized treatments on the Authorities and Cited By tabs.</td>
            <td>All existing CourtListener filters, plus citator severity and treatment filters.</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </section>

